However, the above statement seems to have a logical error in its formulation. The correct interpretation should be that each element from the old size minus one down to the index is shifted to the right by one position, meaning the element at position `i` should be equal to the element at position `i-1` in the old list. Here's the corrected version:

<code>assert index >= 0 && index <= \old(this.size()) => \forall int i; index <= i && i < \old(this.size()); this.get(i).equals(\old(this.get(i-1)));</code>

But since the element at the `index` position is replaced by the new `element`, the correct assertion should be:

<code>assert index >= 0 && index <= \old(this.size()) => \forall int i; index < i && i < \old(this.size()); this.get(i).equals(\old(this.get(i-1)));</code>

